{"kind":"request","text":"codex: first of the follow-ups from my approval 3a82a7f6, and the one that matters to a real user. Not urgent, and explicitly sequenced after 02e3b0c2 merges.\n\nWHAT I FOUND WHILE REVIEWING. ReplaceBinding derives the outgoing binding itself and replaces whatever it happens to find. There is no exported way to read the binding in force beforehand, and no way to say which one you expected. An application that is not chess can learn what it is about to migrate from only by parsing the text of the ErrUninterpretable message, because programmatically it gets errors.Is and nothing more.\n\nWHY THAT IS WORTH A ROW. The consequence is narrow but real: an operator whose migration evidence covers one transition, say fold one to fold two, can point the command at a repository still sitting at fold zero and move it, believing they have done the thing they reasoned about. Nothing lies to them; they simply had no way to check first. It is immediately detectable, because the result names the actual outgoing version, and recoverable by a further replacement, which is why I approved rather than blocked. But this is precisely the audience hugh's finding was about, and asking them to regex an error string is not an interface.\n\nWHY IT ATTACHES TO THE EXISTING MECHANISM RATHER THAN A NEW ONE. You already compute an expected outgoing binding and compare it inside PostDedup on every CAS attempt. An optional caller-supplied expectation should feed that same comparison, not introduce a parallel check that could drift from it. That is why the conditions say so explicitly.\n\nTHE COMPATIBILITY POINT I CARE ABOUT MOST. Omitting the expectation must behave exactly as today. This is an addition for callers who want to be careful, not a new obligation on callers who already exist, and a head that quietly makes the expectation mandatory would break the surface I just approved.\n\nNothing here reflects on that head. Every property I checked holds, and I said so in detail with mutation evidence.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced AFTER 02e3b0c2 merges; this builds on it and must not delay it. Give a caller a way to learn the binding in force without parsing an error message, and a way to say which outgoing binding it expects. Concretely: export a read of the binding in force for a repository, returning the application name and fold version, or a structured field on ErrUninterpretable carrying both, so errors.As yields them programmatically. Then let ReplaceBinding accept an optional expected outgoing fold version and refuse when the actual outgoing does not match, feeding that expectation into the existing expected value the PostDedup closure already compares, rather than adding a second mechanism. When the expectation is omitted, behaviour must be exactly as it is today, so nothing already written against the current signature changes meaning. Regression tests written from the desired behaviour: a caller can read the binding in force on an uninterpretable repository without string parsing; a replacement carrying a matching expectation proceeds; one carrying a mismatched expectation is refused before anything is written and names both versions; and an omitted expectation behaves as now. Prove mutation-sensitivity by making the expectation check always pass and watching the mismatch assertion fire. Do not weaken any property of 02e3b0c2: the initializing-actor proof, the read-site skip, the identical-binding refusal and the per-attempt PostDedup recheck must all still hold, and their existing tests must still pass unchanged. Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review. Do not merge.","severity":"normal","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}